 "intro": {
  "text": "In the Two Knights Defence Black answers the Italian Game's 3.Bc4 with 3...Nf6, developing a piece and pressing on e4. After the sharp 4.Ng5, attacking f7 with knight and bishop, Black gives up a pawn with 4...d5 and plays 5...Na5, the Polerio Defence: White may be a pawn up, but Black's pieces become active and the game is considered equal.",
  "credit": {
   "source": "Wikibooks, Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bc4/3...Nf6/4. Ng5",
