     "notes": {
      "text": "1...e5 takes space in the centre and controls d4, so White cannot easily keep pawns on both d4 and e4. It also stops e4-e5, so your knight can come to f6. 1...c5 (Sicilian) also controls d4; 1...e6 and 1...c6 prepare ...d5, and 1...d5 attacks e4 at once.",
      "credit": {
       "source": "Wikibooks, Chess Opening Theory/1. e4",
       "url": "https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Chess_Opening_Theory/1._e4",
